{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 30, :comments ["<b>If there be laid on him a ransom.</b> They disagree in chapter four and five (Bava Kamma 40a) regarding whom they evaluate — whether according to the damager or according to the injured party. And it would appear that according to the one who says according to the damager, if the ox killed [two people] belonging to two people, they are liable to pay the ransom of each one of them, for all of them became liable to Heaven. And according to the one who says according to the injured party — they are liable only for the value of the injured party. And it is difficult: if so, what does Rav Naḥman need to ask Rav Aḥa bar Ya'akov whether they are liable to give a full ransom for each one — is this not a dispute of the Tanna'im? Rather, it is certainly true according to the one who says according to the damager, that he certainly must pay two ransoms — in a case where the ox killed an important man worth two hundred [and then] a man worth fifty, for he is liable to pay and it is not reduced if they are divided into two persons, and they are liable to give two ransom-values. And the question of Rav Naḥman is according to the Rabbis, who hold that they evaluate according to the injured party. And so it is implied from the words of Rambam, who ruled in chapter 11 of the Laws of Property Damage regarding the law of the ransom-value that they evaluate the value of the slain person, and regarding an ox of two partners he ruled (there chapter 10) that they are liable to give a full ransom to each one. And understand this. And the reason that Rambam ruled stringently is because it is a case of doubtful ransom of a soul [lifnei HaShem]." "<b>And if</b> the blood-avenger arrived first and killed this one before he gives the ransom of his soul, it appears to me that he is killed on account of it [i.e., the killing of the avenger is himself punishable], for the verse exempted only the killing of a hand-killer [acting] unintentionally under the law of 'his heart shall be moved' (יֵחַם לְבָבוֹ), but if his ox killed there is no 'his heart shall be moved' as with killing by hand."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {0 "The last phrase reads literally 'for it constitutes a doubt [of] ransom of soul'; the phrase כופר נפש is a legal term for the ransom paid by the owner of a killer ox.", 1 "Thelogic is compressed: he appears to argue that the blood-avenger who kills the negligent owner before ransom is paid would himself be liable, because the biblical exemption for the avenger (Deut. 19:6, 'his heart shall be moved') applies to hand-killing, not to ox-killing."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:32:16.945006748Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}